Recommended textbooksThese texts complement the notebooks at different levels. Start with Shearer or
Stein and Wysession; use Aki and Richards or Dahlen and Tromp when you want a
more mathematical treatment.Shearer, P. M. (2019). Introduction to Seismology (3rd ed.). Cambridge University Press. A clear first textbook on seismic waves, earthquakes, seismic sources, and imaging.Stein, S., & Wysession, M. (2003). An Introduction to Seismology, Earthquakes, and Earth Structure. Blackwell Publishing. A broad, physical introduction connecting seismology to plate tectonics and Earth structure.Aki, K., & Richards, P. G. (2002). Quantitative Seismology (2nd ed.). University Science Books. The standard advanced reference for wave propagation, source theory, and seismic observations.Dahlen, F. A., & Tromp, J. (1998). Theoretical Global Seismology. Princeton University Press. A rigorous reference for normal modes, ray theory, and global seismic wavefields.Lowrie, W., & Fichtner, A. (2020). Fundamentals of Geophysics (3rd ed.). Cambridge University Press. Useful for placing seismology alongside gravity, magnetics, geodesy, and other geophysical methods.Other ResourcesLive Jupyter Notebooks for Seismology
